TRANSFORMER PUZZLES
===================

  My solutions to Sasha Rush's Transformer Puzzles.

      https://github.com/srush/Transformer-Puzzles

  Transformer Puzzles are coding puzzles based on the internals of
  the Transformer. Each asks you to reimplement a small sequence
  operation using only RASP-style attention primitives.


RULES
-----

  1. Like a transformer, length is fixed. Operations act on the
     entire sequence in parallel.

  2. There is a global indices giving each position. Use where to
     branch on a condition, as in NumPy or PyTorch.

  3. The main operation is attention: build a selector from key and
     query, then aggregate with .value(...).

  4. Solutions may use earlier puzzle functions.

  Bodies below are the function body only, including return.


SOLUTIONS
---------

  0  head

      return (key(indices) == query(0)).value(seq)

  1  index

      return (key(indices) == query(i)).value(seq)

  2  shift

      return (key(indices) == query(indices - i)).value(
          seq, default=default
      )

  3  ralign

      has_non_pad_after = (
          (query("_") != key(seq)) & (key(indices) > query(indices))
      ).value(1)
      cut = (
          (key(has_non_pad_after) == query(0))
          & (query("_") == key(seq))
      ).value(1)
      return shift(cut, ldefault, seq)

  4  split

      i = (query(v) == key(seq)).value(indices)
      blank = "0" if not get_first_part else "_"
      x = where(
          (i > indices) if get_first_part else (i < indices),
          seq,
          blank,
      )
      return ralign("0", x)
